# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Arca — environment
# Copy to `.env.local`. Every value here is optional: with none of them set the
# app boots against the in-memory fixture repository, which is how you develop
# the UI without a database. See backend/db/repository.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PostgreSQL. Set this and the app switches from fixtures to the real
# object-graph schema. Vercel Postgres and Neon both hand you this string.
#
# IMPORTANT: setting this is a commitment, not a preference. The app does not
# fall back to fixtures when the database is unreachable — falling back would
# mean a running app quietly serving different data than the one it was
# configured for, and a sign-in that "worked" against a store that forgets
# everything on restart. It says the database is down instead.
#
# So if you set this, the database has to be up:
#
#   docker compose up -d      # start it
#   npm run db:migrate        # create the tables
#   npm run db:seed           # the campaign, its members and their inventory
#
# Unset it (or comment it out) to go back to fixtures.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://arca:arca@127.0.0.1:5432/arca

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in
#
# There is nothing to configure. Arca has no OAuth provider and no mail server:
# sign-in is an email address and a password (backend/lib/password.ts), and
# there are two ways to get an account.
#
#   /signup   anyone with the link, and it always produces a PLAYER.
#   /members  the GM, who may seat a player OR another GM.
#
# Neither sends mail. There is no address confirmation and no password-reset
# link, because sending either needs an SMTP provider and credentials this app
# deliberately does not carry. Recovery is the GM clearing a password from
# /members, which puts that member back at "choose a password" — the same state
# a newly added one starts in. As one statement, if you would rather not sign
# in as the GM:
#
#   UPDATE users SET password_hash = NULL WHERE email = 'kova@ravenholt.example';
#
# With fixtures (no DATABASE_URL) every member starts unenrolled and the whole
# store resets whenever the server restarts, which is why `npm run dev` needs
# no database and no configuration at all — and why nothing you do in that mode
# is kept.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The deployment's DEFAULT campaign (backend/lib/campaign.ts). Users can create
# and switch between several campaigns from the campaign menu; this one is where
# /signup lands new players, what `npm run db:seed` writes, and the fallback for
# a signed-in user with no campaign selected. Leave unset to use the seeded id.
# ARCA_CAMPAIGN_ID=00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Session cookie signing key (backend/lib/session-token.ts). At least 32
# characters; shorter makes the app refuse to start. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Left unset, the key is derived from DATABASE_URL (or is random per process in
# fixture mode). That works, but rotating the database password then signs
# everybody out — so SET THIS IN PRODUCTION. Changing it signs everybody out.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ARCA_SESSION_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The real GM's address — the "Xen" account in backend/db/seed-data.ts.
#
# Set this BEFORE running `npm run db:seed`, because the seed writes the
# address as it stands at that moment. Left unset, the account is seeded as
# xen@ravenholt.example, which is RFC 2606 reserved and can never receive mail
# — harmless, but not an address anybody can be reached at.
#
# It lives here rather than in seed-data.ts because this repository is public,
# and an address committed to a public file stays in the git history after it
# is edited out. Nothing about it is secret; it simply should not be scraped.
#
# NO PASSWORD GOES WITH IT in the repo. The account is seeded unenrolled like
# every other member and claims its password on first sign-in. On a PUBLIC
# deployment that window is worth closing quickly: an unenrolled address is
# claimable by whoever reaches /signin first, so enrol it as soon as the app is
# up — or close it with `npm run db:make-gm` below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ARCA_GM_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Optional, read only by `npm run db:make-gm -- <Name>` (backend/db/make-gm.ts),
# the non-destructive way to make ARCA_GM_EMAIL a GM on a live database. If set,
# it is scrypt-hashed and stored so the account is usable at once; unset, the
# owner sets a password on first sign-in. Put it in your shell or .env.local for
# that one command, then remove it — never commit it, never set it on Vercel.
# ARCA_GM_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tests only. backend/db/catalog.postgres.test.ts runs only when this names a
# THROWAWAY local database: every test reseeds, and reseeding deletes the
# campaign. It never reads DATABASE_URL, and refuses non-loopback hosts.
#
#   docker exec arca-postgres createdb -U arca arca_test
#   ARCA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://arca:arca@127.0.0.1:5432/arca_test npm test
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ARCA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://arca:arca@127.0.0.1:5432/arca_test
